{
  "data": {
    "A": "Develop and validate the Kitamasa-style polynomial approach: reduce powers modulo x^N − ΣP_j x^(N−j), exponentiate x^(H−1), then combine the coefficients with A. This replaces dense O(N³ log H) matrix products with O(N² log H) work. Preserve the recurrence’s coefficient orientation and modular semantics; check edge cases such as N=1 and powers at or below the base-term range. Use the measured polynomial candidate as a starting point, not as assumed-correct code.",
    "B": "Independently optimize the polynomial multiplication/reduction kernel, without repeating dense-matrix loop transformations. Investigate bounded batches of residue products in 64-bit accumulation, zero-factor skips, and scratch-buffer/layout costs; prove overflow bounds for every accumulation and reduction before retaining an optimization. Compare against the polynomial candidate on the full valid input range, and keep changes only when fresh measurements show a benefit.",
    "C": "Explore parallelism in the polynomial method for the 4-core ARM64 target. A safe lane is to compute independent convolution output coefficients in parallel, then perform the high-degree recurrence reduction in its required descending order; avoid concurrent scatter updates or other data races. Use a size threshold to avoid overhead on small inputs, preserve the existing build setup, and measure the complete program. The measured OpenMP dense-matrix variants do not justify carrying that approach over unchanged.",
    "G": "Integrate only tested contributions from A/B/C. The measured polynomial candidate (A, 0.00568 s) is the strongest reported result; the baseline is 0.01987 s, while reported matrix-loop candidates range from 0.00624 to 0.01030 s. Validate candidate correctness before selection—the supplied observations establish baseline correctness but do not report correctness for each candidate. Measure the integrated program afresh rather than adding expected gains; retain only correct changes that improve the complete run. Do not specialize to observed test values.",
    "analysis": "The recurrence admits polynomial reduction, avoiding the baseline’s repeated dense matrix multiplication. The measured polynomial implementation is substantially faster than the correct baseline, while the matrix-loop and OpenMP experiments are slower than that candidate. Candidate correctness is not stated in the measurements, so verification and fresh end-to-end integration measurements are essential. All improvements must preserve results for every valid input."
